	$pages = array(
		// Pillar and method.
		array( 'path' => 'the-islamic-dilemma', 'title' => 'The Islamic Dilemma, Answered', 'template' => $section, 'pattern' => 'islamic-dilemma-page', 'excerpt' => 'The Islamic Dilemma, answered: what the argument claims, why it fails, and the strongest Christian reply.' ),
		array( 'path' => 'method', 'title' => 'The measure', 'pattern' => 'criterion-statement', 'excerpt' => 'The criterion this website uses to weigh every claim.' ),
		array( 'path' => 'corrections', 'title' => 'Corrections log', 'pattern' => 'corrections-log', 'excerpt' => 'Every error found, what the page said, and what it says now.' ),
		array( 'path' => 'answers', 'title' => 'FAQs', 'template' => $section, 'pattern' => 'answers-page', 'excerpt' => 'The questions people search for most, answered and labelled.' ),

		// The Four Gospels.
		array( 'path' => 'gospels', 'title' => 'The Four Gospels', 'template' => $section, 'pattern' => 'section-gospels', 'excerpt' => 'Mark, Matthew, Luke and John examined: authorship, sources, dates and discrepancies.' ),
		array(
			'path' => 'gospels/mark', 'title' => 'Mark, the first gospel', 'excerpt' => 'Anonymous, written around 70 CE, and used by both Matthew and Luke.',
			'body' => array(
				'@consensus',
				'Mark is the shortest gospel and, on the dominant scholarly view, the first written. The text names no author. The name “Mark” comes from church tradition, first recorded by Papias of Hierapolis in the early second century and preserved by Eusebius (Ecclesiastical History 3.39).',
				'## Date and setting',
				'Most scholars place Mark between 65 and 75 CE, close to the Jewish War and the destruction of the Temple in 70 CE. The Greek is plain and the narrative moves quickly: no birth story, no resurrection appearance in the earliest manuscripts.',
				'## Where it ends',
				'The two oldest complete Greek Bibles, Codex Sinaiticus and Codex Vaticanus, both of the fourth century, end the gospel at 16:8 with the women fleeing the empty tomb. Verses 16:9-20 appear in later manuscripts and differ in style and vocabulary. Most modern translations mark them as a later addition.',
				'## Why it matters here',
				'Matthew and Luke both rework Mark. Anything they add or change is therefore visible, and the changes tell us how the early community handled its source. See <a href="/gospels/synoptic-problem/">the synoptic problem</a>.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Bruce Metzger, <em>A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament</em>, notes on Mark 16:9-20.',
				'- Raymond Brown, <em>An Introduction to the New Testament</em>, chapter on Mark.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'gospels/matthew', 'title' => 'Matthew and the law', 'excerpt' => 'Mark rewritten for a Jewish-Christian community: a genealogy, a nativity, and a law that does not pass away.',
			'body' => array(
				'@consensus',
				'Matthew reproduces about ninety percent of Mark, adds a large body of teaching shared with Luke (the hypothetical source Q), and supplies material found only here. It was written in Greek, not translated from Hebrew, and is usually dated between 80 and 90 CE.',
				'## The genealogy and the nativity',
				'The gospel opens with a genealogy of Jesus through Joseph (1:1-17), then a nativity centred on Joseph: the dream, the magi, the flight to Egypt. Luke tells a different story, centred on Mary. The two accounts agree only that Jesus was born in Bethlehem of a virgin named Mary, in the days of Herod.',
				'## The law',
				'Matthew 5:17-19 has Jesus say that he has not come to abolish the law, and that not one stroke of it will pass until heaven and earth pass away. No other gospel contains the saying. It matters for the question of Paul of Tarsus, who writes that Christ is the end of the law (Romans 10:4).',
				'## Sources',
				'- Raymond Brown, <em>An Introduction to the New Testament</em>.',
				'- Ulrich Luz, <em>Matthew 1-7: A Commentary</em>.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'gospels/luke', 'title' => 'Luke and his sources', 'excerpt' => 'A preface that admits earlier accounts, a second nativity, and a sequel in Acts.',
			'body' => array(
				'@consensus',
				'Luke is the only gospel to open with a preface in the manner of Greek historians (1:1-4). It says that many had already undertaken to compile an account, that the author followed things closely from the beginning, and that he writes an orderly account for Theophilus.',
				'## What the preface admits',
				'The author is not an eyewitness. He received the tradition from those who were, and he drew on earlier written accounts. That agrees with the synoptic evidence: Luke uses Mark and the sayings source shared with Matthew.',
				'## Luke and Acts',
				'Luke and Acts are one work in two volumes by the same author, written around 80 to 90 CE. Acts is the only narrative of Paul of Tarsus’s career apart from his own letters, and the two do not always agree. See <a href="/paul/acts-galatians/">Acts against Galatians</a>.',
				'## The genealogy',
				'Luke traces Jesus through Joseph back to Adam (3:23-38). The list differs from Matthew’s in almost every name between David and Joseph, including the name of Joseph’s father (Heli in Luke, Jacob in Matthew).',
				'## Sources',
				'- Joseph Fitzmyer, <em>The Gospel According to Luke</em>, Anchor Bible.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'gospels/john', 'title' => 'John and the Jesus who speaks of himself', 'excerpt' => 'The latest gospel, and the only one in which Jesus makes plain claims about his own nature.',
			'body' => array(
				'@consensus',
				'John is usually dated between 90 and 100 CE and differs from the other three in structure, vocabulary and theology. It has no birth narrative, no exorcisms, and no parables. Jesus speaks at length about himself: the “I am” sayings, the discourses, and the prayer of chapter 17.',
				'## The prologue',
				'John 1:1-18 identifies the Word (Logos) who was with God and was God, and who became flesh. This is the clearest statement of pre-existence in the four gospels. It is not found in Mark, which is the earliest.',
				'## Two voices in one gospel',
				'John also preserves sayings that point the other way: “the Father is greater than I” (14:28) and “this is eternal life, that they know you, the only true God” (17:3). Christian tradition reads these as speaking of the Son’s role. The Islamic reading takes them at face value.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Raymond Brown, <em>The Gospel According to John</em>, Anchor Bible.',
				'- Raymond Brown, <em>An Introduction to the New Testament</em>.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'gospels/thomas', 'title' => 'The Gospel of Thomas', 'excerpt' => 'A sayings collection with no narrative: what it is, when it was written, and where its sayings are used in this gospel.',
			'body' => array(
				'@consensus',
				'The Gospel of Thomas is a collection of 114 sayings attributed to Jesus. It has no birth story, no miracles, no arrest, no crucifixion and no resurrection. Its opening line names the writer as Didymos Judas Thomas, and the name is a claim of authority, not evidence of authorship.',
				'## Manuscripts',
				'The text survives complete only in Coptic, in a codex found at Nag Hammadi in Egypt in 1945 and copied in the fourth century. Three Greek fragments from Oxyrhynchus, copied around 200 CE, show that the work is older than the Coptic copy.',
				'## Date and sources',
				'Scholars date the work anywhere from the late first century to the middle of the second, and they divide on whether it depends on the canonical gospels or preserves independent tradition. About half its sayings have a parallel in Matthew, Mark or Luke. The rest are unique to Thomas, and many carry an ascetic and mystical tone that the Qur’an does not share.',
				'@minority',
				'A minority of scholars hold that Thomas preserves sayings older than the canonical gospels. Others, such as Nicholas Perrin, argue that it was composed from them and from a harmony of the four, the Diatessaron.',
				'## How it is used here',
				'The sayings of Thomas are not set out on a page of their own. Those that agree with the Qur’an as the measure, and those that are beyond it or unclear, are woven into the chapters of <a href="/gospel-according-to-islam/">The Gospel According to Islam</a>, each with its saying number, a colour mark and a note. 44 sayings that conflict with the Qur’an or only repeat another are listed, with the reason for each, on <a href="/gospels/thomas/set-aside/">the page of sayings set aside</a>.',
				'## The colour marks',
				'- [agree] Agrees with the Qur’an as the measure.',
				'- [beyond] Beyond the measure: the Qur’an says nothing either way. Included, and marked.',
				'- [unclear] Unclear: the text or its sense is uncertain. Included, and marked.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Thomas O. Lambdin (trans.), “The Gospel of Thomas”, in <em>The Nag Hammadi Library in English</em>, ed. James M. Robinson, 3rd ed. (Leiden: E. J. Brill, 1988). Saying numbers here follow this edition; the wording used in the chapters is our own digest of it.',
				'- Helmut Koester, <em>Ancient Christian Gospels</em>.',
				'- Nicholas Perrin, <em>Thomas and Tatian</em>.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'gospels/thomas/set-aside', 'title' => 'Sayings of Thomas set aside', 'excerpt' => 'The sayings of the Gospel of Thomas that are not used in this gospel, each with the reason.',
			'body' => array(
				'Every saying of the Gospel of Thomas was tested against the Qur’an as the measure (see <a href="/method/">the measure</a>). 70 are used in <a href="/gospel-according-to-islam/">The Gospel According to Islam</a>, each with a colour mark and a note. The other 44 are listed here with the reason for each: 37 conflict with the Qur’an and 7 only repeat another saying.',
				'## The colour marks',
				'- [agree] Agrees with the Qur’an as the measure.',
				'- [beyond] Beyond the measure: the Qur’an says nothing either way. Included, and marked.',
				'- [unclear] Unclear: the text or its sense is uncertain. Included, and marked.',
				'- [conflict] Conflicts with the Qur’an: the saying says what the Qur’an denies, or denies what it affirms. Not used.',
				'- [notneeded] Not needed: it repeats a saying that is already used. Not used.',
				'## A note on “Father”',
				'Thomas often says “the Father” where Matthew says “heaven” or Mark says “God” (compare Mark 3:35 with Thomas 99, and Matthew 13:33 with Thomas 96). Where the canonical parallel makes the same point in other words, the saying is used and the note says so. A saying is set aside only when “Father” carries a claim of sonship or shared nature, which the Qur’an rejects (5:18; 19:35; 112:3). The Qur’an itself does not call God “Father”, so this is a reading and not a settled rule.',
				'## Sayings set aside',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 1.</strong> The one who finds what these sayings mean will not taste death. Promises that whoever finds the meaning of the sayings will not taste death. The Qur’an says every soul will taste death (3:185).',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 2.</strong> Seekers must keep seeking. Finding brings distress, then wonder, and then rule over everything. Seeking ends in ruling over the All. Rule over all things belongs to God alone (3:26; 67:1).',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 3.</strong> The kingdom is not in the sky or the sea. It is within and around you. Knowing yourselves is how you learn you are children of the living Father. Tells the hearers they are children of the living Father. The Qur’an rejects the claim to be God’s children (5:18).',
				'- [notneeded] <strong>Thomas 5.</strong> See what is in front of you and the hidden will be shown. Nothing hidden stays hidden. Parallel to Mark 4:22 and Luke 8:17. It adds nothing the chapters need.',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 6.</strong> Asked about fasting, prayer, almsgiving and diet, Jesus answers only: do not lie, and do not do what you hate. Everything hidden will be uncovered. Answers the disciples’ questions on fasting, prayer and alms with no command. The Qur’an prescribes all three (2:183; 2:43; 2:110).',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 12.</strong> The disciples ask who will lead them after Jesus leaves. He names James the righteous, for whom heaven and earth were made. Names James as the one for whose sake heaven and earth came to be. The Qur’an says heaven and earth were created in truth, and names no person as their cause (44:38-39; 51:56).',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 14.</strong> Fasting, prayer and almsgiving are said to bring sin, condemnation and harm. Disciples should eat what is set before them and heal the sick. What comes out of the mouth defiles. (Compare Matthew 15:11; Luke 10:8-9.) Says fasting, prayer and almsgiving bring sin, condemnation and harm. The Qur’an prescribes them (2:183; 2:43; 2:110). The closing lines on food echo Mark 7:15 and Luke 10:8-9.',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 15.</strong> When you see one not born of woman, bow down and worship, for that one is your father. Commands worship of one “not born of woman” and calls him father. Worship is for God alone (17:23; 3:79-80).',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 17.</strong> Jesus will give what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, no hand has touched, and no mind has imagined. Jesus speaks as the giver of what no eye has seen. The Qur’an says that gift is from God alone (32:17).',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 19.</strong> Blessed is the one who existed before coming into being. Disciples who listen will be served by stones and will have five trees in Paradise that never lose their leaves. “Blessed is he who came into being before he came into being” implies pre-existence. The Qur’an likens ʿĪsā to Adam, created from dust (3:59).',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 22.</strong> Infants at the breast show who enters the kingdom. Entry comes when two become one: inside and outside, above and below, male and female. Says male and female must become one “so that the male not be male nor the female female”. The Qur’an affirms both sexes as God’s creation (49:13; 53:45).',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 28.</strong> Jesus stood in the world and appeared in flesh. He found all drunk and none thirsty, and he grieved for their blindness. When they shake off the wine they will repent. Says Jesus “appeared in flesh”, the language of pre-existence. The Qur’an calls him a messenger, born of Maryam (5:75).',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 30.</strong> Where there are three gods they are gods. Where there are two or one, Jesus says, he is with them. “Where there are three gods, they are gods.” The Qur’an forbids saying “three” (4:171; 5:73).',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 37.</strong> The disciples ask when Jesus will be revealed. It will be when they strip without shame, trample their clothes like children, and then see the son of the living one and have no fear. Undressing without shame in order to see “the son of the living one”. The Qur’an commands modesty (24:30-31; 7:26) and rejects divine sonship (19:35).',
				'- [notneeded] <strong>Thomas 41.</strong> Whoever has will be given more. Whoever has nothing will lose even what little there is. (Compare Mark 4:25.) A proverb that the one who has will be given more (Mark 4:25). Not needed.',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 43.</strong> Asked who he is, Jesus says they have become like the Jews, who love the tree and hate its fruit, or love the fruit and hate the tree. Likens the disciples to “the Jews” who love the tree and hate its fruit. The Qur’an does not judge a whole people alike (3:113). It also answers “who are you?” by rebuke.',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 44.</strong> Blasphemy against the Father or the Son will be forgiven. Blasphemy against the holy spirit will not be forgiven on earth or in heaven. (Compare Matthew 12:31-32.) Lists Father, Son and holy spirit together. Matthew 12:31-32 and Luke 12:10 do not, and the Qur’an rejects a triad (4:171).',
				'- [notneeded] <strong>Thomas 48.</strong> If two make peace in one house, they can tell the mountain to move and it will move. (Compare Matthew 18:19; 17:20.) Parallel to Matthew 18:19 and 17:20, and repeated in saying 106. Not needed.',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 49.</strong> Blessed are the solitary and the chosen, who come from the kingdom and will return to it. Blesses the “solitary and elect”, who come from the kingdom and return to it. The Qur’an says monasticism was not prescribed (57:27).',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 50.</strong> When asked where you came from, say from the light, which came into being by itself. When asked who you are, say you are its children, the chosen of the living Father. The sign of the Father in you is movement and rest. Says the elect came from the light and are children of the living Father. See 5:18. The story of origin is Thomas’s own.',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 51.</strong> The disciples ask when the dead will rest and the new world come. Jesus says what they await has already come and they have not recognised it. Says the new world has already come and is not recognised. The Qur’an teaches that the Hour is still to come (7:187; 31:34).',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 52.</strong> The disciples say that twenty-four prophets spoke in Israel and all spoke of Jesus. He says they ignored the living one before them and spoke only of the dead. Says the twenty-four prophets spoke “in” Jesus and calls them “the dead”. The Qur’an honours the earlier prophets and makes no distinction among them (2:136).',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 53.</strong> Asked if circumcision is useful, Jesus says that if it were, children would be born circumcised. True circumcision is in the spirit. Treats circumcision as of the spirit only. The prophetic tradition counts circumcision among the acts of fiṭra (Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī, Book of Dress).',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 55.</strong> Whoever does not hate father and mother, brothers and sisters, and carry the cross, cannot be a disciple. (Compare Luke 14:26-27.) Whoever does not hate father and mother cannot be a disciple (Luke 14:26). The Qur’an commands kindness to parents (17:23-24; 31:14-15). Matthew 10:37 has the softer form.',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 56.</strong> Whoever has understood the world has found a corpse, and the world is not worthy of such a person. Calls the world a corpse. The Qur’an calls its good things a provision and forbids only excess (28:77; 7:32).',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 61.</strong> Two will rest on one couch and one will die and one will live. Salome asks who he is. He is the one from the undivided, and one who is whole will be filled with light but one who is divided with darkness. Salome at the couch. Jesus says “I am he who exists from the undivided”, a claim of shared nature with the Father (112:3). The text is damaged.',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 64.</strong> A host sends his servant to invite guests to dinner. Each makes an excuse of business, a house, a wedding or a farm, so the host invites those found in the streets. Buyers and merchants will not enter the Father’s places. (Compare Luke 14:16-24.) The banquet (Luke 14:16-24). Thomas adds that “businessmen and merchants will not enter the places of my father”, which Luke does not say. The Qur’an permits trade (2:275).',
				'- [notneeded] <strong>Thomas 72.</strong> A man asks Jesus to tell his brothers to divide the inheritance. Jesus asks who made him a divider. (Compare Luke 12:13-14.) Parallel to Luke 12:13-14. The Qur’an sets its own rules of inheritance (4:11). Not needed.',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 75.</strong> Many stand at the door, but only the solitary will enter the bridal chamber. Only the solitary will enter the bridal chamber. See saying 49 and Qur’an 57:27.',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 77.</strong> Jesus is the light above all and the all. Split a piece of wood and he is there, lift a stone and you find him. Jesus says he is the light above all and the All, and that he is in split wood and a lifted stone. The Qur’an reserves these to God (24:35; 57:4).',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 80.</strong> Whoever has known the world has found the body, and the world is not worthy of such a person. As in saying 56, with the body in place of the corpse (28:77; 7:32).',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 82.</strong> Whoever is near Jesus is near the fire, and whoever is far from him is far from the kingdom. Whoever is near Jesus is near the fire. The Qur’an makes nearness to God depend on obedience (3:31).',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 85.</strong> Adam came from great power and wealth yet was not worthy of you, for otherwise he would not have tasted death. Says Adam came from great power yet was unworthy and so died. The Qur’an says Adam was made from clay, was forgiven, and died as all do (2:37; 3:59).',
				'- [notneeded] <strong>Thomas 91.</strong> Asked who he is, Jesus says they can read the face of sky and earth but have not recognised the one in front of them. Asked who he is, Jesus rebukes the hearers (compare Luke 12:54-56). Not needed.',
				'- [notneeded] <strong>Thomas 93.</strong> Do not give what is holy to dogs or throw pearls to swine. (Compare Matthew 7:6.) Parallel to Matthew 7:6. Not needed.',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 100.</strong> Shown a gold coin and told Caesar demands taxes, Jesus says to give Caesar what is Caesar’s, God what is God’s, and to him what is his. (Compare Mark 12:13-17.) The coin and Caesar (Mark 12:13-17). Thomas adds “give me what is mine”, which sets Jesus beside God.',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 101.</strong> Whoever does not hate father and mother as Jesus does cannot be his disciple, and whoever does not love them as he does likewise. His true mother gave him life. Hate father and mother “as I do”. See saying 55 and Qur’an 17:23.',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 105.</strong> Whoever knows the father and the mother will be called the son of a harlot. Whoever knows the father and the mother will be called the son of a harlot. The Qur’an clears Maryam of any such charge (19:28-33; 4:156).',
				'- [notneeded] <strong>Thomas 106.</strong> When you make the two one you become sons of man, and when you tell the mountain to move it will move. (Compare Matthew 17:20.) As in saying 48. Not needed.',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 108.</strong> Whoever drinks from Jesus’ mouth will become like him, and what is hidden will be revealed. Whoever drinks from Jesus’ mouth will become like him, and he will become that person. The Qur’an says ʿĪsā is a man, like Adam (3:59).',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 109.</strong> The kingdom is like a man whose field hides treasure that he does not know about. His son sells the field. The buyer finds the treasure and lends money at interest. (Compare Matthew 13:44.) The hidden treasure ends with the buyer lending at interest. The Qur’an forbids it (2:275), and Matthew 13:44 has no such ending.',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 110.</strong> Whoever finds the world and becomes rich should renounce the world. Renounce the world. See saying 56 (28:77).',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 111.</strong> The heavens and earth will roll up before you, and whoever lives from the living one will not see death. The world is not worthy of one who finds himself. Whoever lives from the living one will not see death. See saying 1 (3:185).',
				'- [conflict] <strong>Thomas 114.</strong> Peter says that Mary should leave because women are not worthy of life. Jesus says he will lead her so she may become male, for every woman who makes herself male will enter the kingdom of heaven. Says Mary must be made male to enter the kingdom. The Qur’an honours Maryam as chosen above the women of the worlds (3:42).',
				'## Source and credit',
				'- Thomas O. Lambdin (trans.), “The Gospel of Thomas”, in <em>The Nag Hammadi Library in English</em>, ed. James M. Robinson, 3rd ed. (Leiden: E. J. Brill, 1988). The summaries above are our own wording.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'gospels/synoptic-problem', 'title' => 'The synoptic problem', 'excerpt' => 'Why Matthew, Mark and Luke share whole sentences, and what Markan priority and Q explain.',
			'body' => array(
				'@consensus',
				'Matthew, Mark and Luke agree in order, wording and even in rare Greek words, far beyond what independent accounts of the same events would produce. Some literary relationship exists. The question of which way it runs is the synoptic problem.',
				'## Markan priority',
				'Most scholars hold that Mark was written first and that Matthew and Luke each used it. Matthew and Luke seldom agree against Mark in order, and Mark’s rougher Greek is more easily explained as the original than as a simplification.',
				'## Q',
				'About 230 verses are shared by Matthew and Luke but absent from Mark, chiefly sayings. The usual explanation is a second source, called Q (German <em>Quelle</em>, “source”). No copy of Q survives.',
				'@minority',
				'A minority of scholars reject Q. The Farrer hypothesis holds that Luke used Matthew as well as Mark. The older Griesbach hypothesis holds that Mark abbreviated Matthew and Luke. Both remove the need for Q.',
				'## Why it matters here',
				'On any of these solutions the gospels are not independent eyewitness reports. They are edited works that depend on earlier written sources.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Mark Goodacre, <em>The Synoptic Problem: A Way Through the Maze</em>.',
				'- Bart Ehrman, <em>The New Testament: A Historical Introduction to the Early Christian Writings</em>.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'gospels/discrepancies', 'title' => 'The discrepancy tables', 'excerpt' => 'Genealogies, nativity, Last Supper, crucifixion and resurrection, set side by side with references.',
			'body' => array(
				'Each table sets the four gospels side by side, with chapter and verse, and records only what the texts say. Interpretation is kept apart from the table.',
				'- <a href="/gospels/discrepancies/resurrection/">The resurrection accounts</a>: who goes to the tomb, who is there, what is said, and where Jesus appears.',
				'More tables will be added here as they are completed: the genealogies (Matthew 1 and Luke 3), the nativity, the Last Supper and the crucifixion. Every new table is announced in the <a href="/corrections/">corrections log</a> if it changes an existing page.',
			),
		),
		array( 'path' => 'gospels/discrepancies/resurrection', 'title' => 'The resurrection accounts compared', 'template' => 'page-wide', 'pattern' => 'discrepancy-resurrection', 'excerpt' => 'The four resurrection accounts set side by side, with references.' ),

		// Paul of Tarsus.
		array( 'path' => 'paul', 'title' => 'Paul of Tarsus', 'template' => $section, 'pattern' => 'section-paul', 'excerpt' => 'Paul of Tarsus against the men who knew ʿĪsā ibn Maryam: his letters, the account in Acts, and the Jerusalem church.' ),
		array(
			'path' => 'paul/acts-galatians', 'title' => 'Acts against Galatians', 'excerpt' => 'Two accounts of the same years that cannot both be right: Damascus, Jerusalem, and the council.',
			'body' => array(
				'@consensus',
				'Paul of Tarsus describes his early career in Galatians 1-2. Luke describes it in Acts 9-15. The two cover the same ground and differ at several points.',
				'## After the conversion',
				'Galatians 1:15-17: Paul of Tarsus says he did not consult anyone but went away to Arabia and then returned to Damascus. Acts 9:19-25 has him spend days with the disciples in Damascus and preach in the synagogues, with no journey to Arabia.',
				'## The first visit to Jerusalem',
				'Galatians 1:18-20: after three years he went up to Jerusalem to visit Cephas, stayed fifteen days, and saw no other apostle except James. Acts 9:26-30: Barnabas brings him to the apostles, and he moves freely among them in Jerusalem.',
				'## The council',
				'Galatians 2:1-10 reports a meeting fourteen years later with Barnabas and Titus, at which the leaders “added nothing” to him. Acts 15 reports a council at which James proposes a decree: gentiles are to abstain from idol meat, blood, strangled animals and sexual immorality. Paul of Tarsus never mentions the decree in his letters, and in 1 Corinthians 8-10 he treats idol meat as a matter of conscience.',
				'## How scholars handle it',
				'Where the two disagree, historians generally prefer Paul of Tarsus’s own letters to the later account in Acts, since Paul of Tarsus writes first-hand and within his own lifetime. This page rests on that rule.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Hans Dieter Betz, <em>Galatians</em>, Hermeneia.',
				'- Joseph Fitzmyer, <em>The Acts of the Apostles</em>, Anchor Bible.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'paul/paul-against-jesus', 'title' => 'Paul of Tarsus against Jesus', 'excerpt' => 'On the law, on sonship, and on atonement, set against the teaching recorded in the gospels.',
			'body' => array(
				'@islamic',
				'The Islamic position, argued here, is that Paul of Tarsus’s teaching departs from the teaching of Jesus recorded in the gospels. Most Christian scholars disagree and hold that Paul of Tarsus faithfully interprets Jesus. Both readings are set out so the reader can judge.',
				'## The law',
				'Matthew 5:17-19: Jesus has not come to abolish the law, and whoever relaxes the least commandment will be called least in the kingdom. Galatians 2:16: no one is justified by works of the law. Romans 10:4: Christ is the end of the law.',
				'## What must one do?',
				'Mark 10:17-19: asked what to do to inherit eternal life, Jesus lists the commandments. Romans 3:28: a person is justified by faith apart from works of the law.',
				'## The Christian reply',
				'Christian interpreters answer that Jesus fulfilled the law and that Paul of Tarsus speaks of the ritual boundary markers (circumcision, food laws) that separated Jew from gentile, not of the moral law. Scholars of the “new perspective” on Paul of Tarsus, such as E. P. Sanders and James Dunn, develop this reading.',
				'## Where this page stands',
				'The Qur’an speaks of Jesus confirming the Torah (3:50; 5:46). On the matter of the law, the Qur’anic portrait is closer to Matthew 5 than to Galatians. That is the argument; the evidence is above.',
				'## Sources',
				'- E. P. Sanders, <em>Paul and Palestinian Judaism</em>.',
				'- James D. G. Dunn, <em>The Theology of Paul the Apostle</em>.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'paul/james', 'title' => 'James and the Jerusalem church', 'excerpt' => 'The brother who led the first community, and what it required of Paul of Tarsus.',
			'body' => array(
				'@consensus',
				'James, called the brother of the Lord, led the Jerusalem community after Jesus. Paul of Tarsus names him among the “pillars” (Galatians 2:9) and the first apostle he met after three years (1:19).',
				'## The men from James',
				'Galatians 2:12 reports that men came “from James” to Antioch, and that Peter withdrew from eating with gentiles. Paul of Tarsus opposed Peter to his face. The episode shows that the Jerusalem leaders did not share Paul of Tarsus’s position on table fellowship.',
				'## Acts 21',
				'Acts 21:18-25 has James and the elders report that thousands of Jews have believed and are all zealous for the law, and that they have heard Paul of Tarsus teaches Jews to forsake Moses. Paul of Tarsus is asked to take part in a purification rite to show that he too lives in observance of the law.',
				'## The death of James',
				'Josephus (Antiquities 20.200) records the stoning of “James, the brother of Jesus who was called Christ” in 62 CE, on the order of the high priest Ananus. Hegesippus, quoted by Eusebius (Ecclesiastical History 2.23), describes James as strictly observant.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Richard Bauckham, <em>Jude and the Relatives of Jesus in the Early Church</em>.',
				'- Josephus, <em>Jewish Antiquities</em>, book 20.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'paul/ebionites', 'title' => 'The Ebionites', 'excerpt' => 'The early Jewish followers of Jesus who kept the law and rejected Paul of Tarsus.',
			'body' => array(
				'@consensus',
				'The Ebionites (from the Hebrew <em>ebyonim</em>, “the poor”) were a Jewish-Christian group known to us chiefly from the church fathers, who opposed them. They are attested from the second century.',
				'## What the fathers report',
				'Irenaeus (Against Heresies 1.26.2) says the Ebionites used only the Gospel of Matthew, rejected Paul of Tarsus as an apostate from the law, practised circumcision and followed Jewish customs. Eusebius (Ecclesiastical History 3.27) says they held Jesus to be a plain man, born of Joseph and Mary.',
				'## What is uncertain',
				'Our sources are hostile and later than the group’s origins. Scholars debate whether the Ebionites descend directly from the Jerusalem church or arose afterwards. No Ebionite writing survives intact.',
				'## Why it matters here',
				'The existence of Jewish followers of Jesus who kept the law and refused Paul of Tarsus shows that his version of the message was contested from the start. See <a href="/paul/james/">James and the Jerusalem church</a>.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Irenaeus, <em>Against Heresies</em>, book 1.',
				'- Eusebius, <em>Ecclesiastical History</em>, book 3.',
				'- Oskar Skarsaune and Reidar Hvalvik (eds.), <em>Jewish Believers in Jesus: The Early Centuries</em>.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'paul/seven-letters', 'title' => 'The seven letters', 'excerpt' => 'Which letters scholars accept as Paul of Tarsus’s own, and why the argument here rests on them alone.',
			'body' => array(
				'@consensus',
				'Thirteen letters carry Paul of Tarsus’s name. Scholars sort them into three groups.',
				'- Undisputed (seven): Romans, 1 Corinthians, 2 Corinthians, Galatians, Philippians, 1 Thessalonians and Philemon.',
				'- Disputed: Ephesians, Colossians and 2 Thessalonians. Scholars divide on authorship.',
				'- Widely held to be later: 1 and 2 Timothy and Titus (the Pastoral Letters).',
				'## The rule used on this website',
				'Where a claim about Paul of Tarsus’s own teaching is made, it rests on the seven undisputed letters. A saying from Ephesians or the Pastorals is not used to show what Paul of Tarsus himself taught. This keeps the argument within ground that critical scholarship broadly accepts.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Raymond Brown, <em>An Introduction to the New Testament</em>.',
				'- Bart Ehrman, <em>Forged: Writing in the Name of God</em>.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'paul/false-apostles', 'title' => 'The false apostles', 'excerpt' => 'Paul of Tarsus’s own charge against his rivals (2 Corinthians 11:13), and who stood on the other side of it.',
			'body' => array(
				'@consensus',
				'In 2 Corinthians 11:13 Paul of Tarsus writes of “false apostles, deceitful workers, disguising themselves as apostles of Christ”. In 11:5 and 12:11 he calls them the “super-apostles”. The rivals are never named.',
				'## Who were they?',
				'Scholars disagree. Some identify them with Jewish-Christian missionaries from Jerusalem, perhaps linked to the “men from James”. Others see itinerant preachers of a different kind. The letter gives only Paul of Tarsus’s side.',
				'## An accusation with two edges',
				'Galatians 1:6-9 curses anyone who preaches a gospel different from his own, and 2:4 speaks of “false brothers”. The charge of falsehood runs in both directions: Paul of Tarsus’s opponents, whose letters are lost, would have said the same of him.',
				'@islamic',
				'The Islamic reading takes the dispute as evidence that the first community was divided over Paul of Tarsus, and that the side which lost the argument did not leave a record. The evidence for that claim is the Ebionite testimony and Acts 21.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Margaret Thrall, <em>The Second Epistle to the Corinthians</em>, International Critical Commentary.',
			),
		),

		// Jesus in the Qur'an.
		array( 'path' => 'quran', 'title' => 'Jesus in the Qur’an', 'template' => $section, 'pattern' => 'section-quran', 'excerpt' => 'What the Qur’an says of ʿĪsā ibn Maryam, passage by passage.' ),
		array(
			'path' => 'quran/birth', 'title' => 'The annunciation and the birth', 'excerpt' => 'The angels and Maryam, the palm tree, and the infant who speaks.',
			'body' => array(
				'@islamic',
				'The Qur’an tells of Maryam in two places. Sūrat Āl ʿImrān (3:42-47) has the angels announce a word from God, whose name is the Messiah, ʿĪsā ibn Maryam. Sūrat Maryam (19:16-34) tells the story in full.',
				'## The announcement',
				'Maryam withdraws to an eastern place. A spirit sent by God appears as a man and announces a pure boy. She asks how, since no man has touched her. The answer is that God creates what He wills (3:47; 19:20-21).',
				'## The birth',
				'The pains of labour bring her to the trunk of a palm tree. She wishes she had died before this. A voice calls to her from beneath: do not grieve, a stream has been placed below you, and the palm will drop ripe dates (19:23-26).',
				'## The infant speaks',
				'She brings the child to her people, who accuse her. She points to him. He says: “I am the servant of God. He has given me the Book and made me a prophet” (19:30). The speech is a defence of Maryam and a statement of what he is.',
				'## Compare',
				'Luke 1-2 and Matthew 1-2 tell of the virgin birth; neither has the palm tree or the infant’s speech. The infant who speaks appears in the Arabic Infancy Gospel, a later text.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Gabriel Said Reynolds, <em>The Qur’ān and Its Biblical Subtext</em>.',
				'- Ṭabarī, <em>Jāmiʿ al-bayān</em>, on 3:42-47 and 19:16-34.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'quran/signs', 'title' => 'The signs', 'excerpt' => 'The bird of clay, the blind and the leper healed, the dead raised, by the permission of God.',
			'body' => array(
				'@islamic',
				'Qur’an 3:49 and 5:110 list the signs given to ʿĪsā. Each is stated to be by the permission of God, and the phrase is repeated for every sign.',
				'- He fashions from clay the shape of a bird and breathes into it, and it becomes a bird.',
				'- He heals the blind from birth and the leper.',
				'- He gives life to the dead.',
				'- He tells people what they eat and what they store in their houses (3:49).',
				'## Compare',
				'The healing of the blind and the lepers, and the raising of the dead, appear in the gospels (Mark 8:22-26; Luke 7:11-17; John 11). The clay birds do not. They appear in the Infancy Gospel of Thomas, a second-century text, where the child Jesus makes sparrows from clay on the Sabbath.',
				'## The refrain',
				'In every case the Qur’an says “by My permission” (5:110) or “by God’s permission” (3:49). The sign is a gift to the messenger, not a power of his own. This is the Qur’anic measure applied to the miracle stories.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Gabriel Said Reynolds, <em>The Qur’ān and Its Biblical Subtext</em>.',
				'- Bart Ehrman, <em>Lost Scriptures</em>, on the Infancy Gospel of Thomas.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'quran/servant', 'title' => 'A servant and a messenger', 'excerpt' => 'What the Qur’an calls him, and what it says he is not.',
			'body' => array(
				'@islamic',
				'The Qur’an names ʿĪsā a servant (<em>ʿabd</em>), a prophet, and a messenger. It also names him the Messiah, a word from God, and a spirit from Him (4:171). It denies that he is the third of three and denies that he is God’s son.',
				'## What he is',
				'- A servant of God: 19:30; 43:59; 4:172.',
				'- A messenger: 3:49; 5:75; 61:6.',
				'- The Messiah, a word from God and a spirit from Him: 4:171.',
				'## What he is not',
				'Those who say that God is the Messiah, son of Maryam, have disbelieved (5:17, 5:72). Those who say that God is the third of three have disbelieved (5:73). Qur’an 4:171 tells the People of the Book not to go beyond bounds in their religion and not to say “three”.',
				'## The final scene',
				'In 5:116-117 God asks ʿĪsā whether he told people to take him and his mother as gods besides God. He replies that he said only what he was commanded: worship God, my Lord and your Lord.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Ṭabarī and Ibn Kathīr, commentary on 4:171 and 5:116-117.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'quran/table', 'title' => 'The table from heaven', 'excerpt' => 'The disciples ask for a sign, and receive it with a warning.',
			'body' => array(
				'@islamic',
				'Sūrat al-Māʾida, “The Table”, takes its name from 5:112-115. The disciples ask whether God can send down a table from heaven. ʿĪsā tells them to fear God. They say they wish to eat from it, to have their hearts at rest, and to bear witness.',
				'## The prayer and the answer',
				'ʿĪsā prays: “O God, our Lord, send down to us a table from heaven to be a festival for the first of us and the last of us, and a sign from You” (5:114). God answers that He will send it down, and that whoever disbelieves after it will receive a punishment such as no one in the worlds has received (5:115).',
				'## Compare',
				'The gospels have the feeding of the multitudes (Mark 6:30-44; 8:1-10) and the Last Supper (Mark 14:22-25). The Qur’an does not say which, if either, it recalls. Commentators have connected the table with each.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Ṭabarī and Ibn Kathīr, commentary on 5:112-115.',
				'- Gabriel Said Reynolds, <em>The Qur’ān and Its Biblical Subtext</em>.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'quran/raised', 'title' => 'The end of his earthly mission', 'excerpt' => 'Neither killed nor crucified, but raised to God.',
			'body' => array(
				'@islamic',
				'Qur’an 4:157-158 says of those who boast that they killed the Messiah, ʿĪsā ibn Maryam, the messenger of God: “They did not kill him, nor did they crucify him, but it was made to appear so to them. … God raised him to Himself.”',
				'## Related verses',
				'- 3:55: “O ʿĪsā, I will take you (<em>mutawaffīka</em>) and raise you to Myself.”',
				'- 5:117: “When You took me (<em>tawaffaytanī</em>), You were the watcher over them.”',
				'- 19:33: “Peace upon me the day I was born, the day I die, and the day I am raised alive.”',
				'## How Muslims have read it',
				'The majority of classical commentators hold that God raised ʿĪsā alive and that someone else was made to look like him. Some early commentators read <em>tawaffā</em> in 3:55 as death, followed by raising. The disagreement concerns the manner, not the denial that he was crucified.',
				'## The historical question',
				'The crucifixion of Jesus under Pontius Pilate is accepted by nearly all historians, including non-Christian ones, as one of the surest facts about him. That is the scholarly consensus and this website states it plainly. The Qur’an’s statement is a different kind of claim, and the two are set side by side here without blending them.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Ṭabarī, <em>Jāmiʿ al-bayān</em>, on 4:157-158.',
				'- Mahmoud Ayoub, “Towards an Islamic Christology II: The Death of Jesus, Reality or Delusion?”, <em>The Muslim World</em> 70 (1980).',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'quran/ahmad', 'title' => 'The good news of Aḥmad', 'excerpt' => 'The messenger he announced.',
			'body' => array(
				'@islamic',
				'Qur’an 61:6 reports that ʿĪsā said to the Children of Israel: “I am the messenger of God to you, confirming what came before me of the Torah and giving good news of a messenger who will come after me, whose name is Aḥmad.”',
				'## The Muslim reading',
				'Muslim scholars have read the verse as foretelling Muḥammad, the name Aḥmad being from the same root as Muḥammad. Ibn Isḥāq, in the earliest biography, connects the promise with the “Paraclete” of John 15:26 (Guillaume, <em>The Life of Muhammad</em>, p. 104).',
				'## The scholarly reading',
				'@consensus',
				'Scholars of the New Testament identify the Paraclete (<em>paraklētos</em>) of John 14-16 with the Holy Spirit, which John 14:26 states directly. Some Muslim writers argue that the original Greek was <em>periklytos</em>, “the praised one”, which would translate Aḥmad. No Greek manuscript has that reading.',
				'## Where this page stands',
				'The verse is the Qur’an’s own witness. The identification with a passage of John rests on a reading the manuscripts do not support. Both points are stated so the reader can weigh them.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Alfred Guillaume (trans.), <em>The Life of Muhammad</em>.',
				'- Raymond Brown, <em>The Gospel According to John XIII-XXI</em>, on the Paraclete.',
			),
		),

		injil_help_quran_page(),

		// The Gospel According to Islam.
		array( 'path' => 'gospel-according-to-islam', 'title' => 'The Gospel According to Islam', 'template' => $section, 'pattern' => 'section-gospel', 'excerpt' => 'A gospel retold with the Qur’an as the measure.' ),
		array( 'path' => 'gospel-according-to-islam/how-composed', 'title' => 'How this gospel was composed', 'pattern' => 'how-composed', 'excerpt' => 'The rules used to compose the gospel: what is kept, what is left out, and why.' ),

		array(
			'path' => 'bonus', 'title' => 'Bonus chapters', 'excerpt' => 'Extra chapters that stand beside The Gospel According to Islam.',
			'body' => array(
				'Chapters that stand beside <a href="/gospel-according-to-islam/">The Gospel According to Islam</a> but are not part of its main telling.',
				'- <a href="/bonus/epistle/">An Epistle unto the Congregation of Paul of Tarsus</a>: a letter to the followers of Paul of Tarsus, in the manner of the Pauline epistles.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'bonus/epistle', 'title' => 'An Epistle unto the Congregation of Paul of Tarsus', 'excerpt' => 'A letter to the followers of Paul of Tarsus, in the manner of the Pauline epistles.',
			'body' => array(
				'@islamic',
				'I, a bondservant set free from the shadow of Paul of Tarsus{{fn:The opening follows Paul of Tarsus’s own: “Paul, a servant of Jesus Christ” (Romans 1:1; Philippians 1:1; Titus 1:1). The author sets the form of the Pauline letter against Paul of Tarsus.}}, who aforetime walked in the darkness of a false revelation and am now returned unto the doctrine undefiled, write unto thee that yet holdest fast to every gospel that hath been bent from its true course: grace and peace be unto thee from God, Most High and Glorious, and from ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ, His servant, whom He hath held in honour.{{fn:“Grace and peace” is Paul of Tarsus’s greeting formula (Romans 1:7; 1 Corinthians 1:3; Galatians 1:3). Here “servant” (<em>ʿabd</em>) is the Qur’anic title: 4:172; 19:30; 43:59.}}',
				'I give thanks unto God, Most High and Glorious, who in His bounty{{fn:Paul of Tarsus’s letters open with a thanksgiving after the greeting (Romans 1:8; 1 Corinthians 1:4; Philippians 1:3).}} hath opened the eyes of some among His servants, and loosed them from the fetters of a faith inherited without understanding. Wherefore I write, not with the pen of malice, but with the ink of love, born of the light of truth that hath visited the heart and stirred the soul.',
				'Thou hast heard how I, in time past, did defend Paul of Tarsus with all my zeal, reading out his letters{{fn:Paul of Tarsus directs that his letters be read aloud to the congregation (Colossians 4:16; 1 Thessalonians 5:27).}} and guarding him against every reproach. Yet when I was tried with proofs and with light, it was made plain unto me that the gospel I had defended was not the Gospel of ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ, but another gospel: not from heaven, but from the tongue of one who professed to have seen a light upon the road{{fn:The Damascus road: Acts 9:3; 22:6; 26:13. Paul of Tarsus’s own letters mention a revelation but give no account of the road (Galatians 1:11-12, 15-16).}}, with no witness, no confirmation, and no testimony of the congregation{{fn:Acts 9:7 says his companions heard the voice and saw no one. Acts 22:9 says they saw the light but did not hear the voice. Paul of Tarsus himself says he did not confer with anyone for three years (Galatians 1:16-18).}}, and who thereupon named himself an apostle of Christ Jesus.',
				'Hast thou forgotten that Paul of Tarsus never met ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ?{{fn:Paul of Tarsus does not claim to have known Jesus in the flesh. He claims to have seen the risen Lord (1 Corinthians 9:1; 15:8) and to have received his gospel “by the revelation of Jesus Christ” (Galatians 1:11-12). Compare 2 Corinthians 5:16.}} That he never sat to hear His sayings? And knowest thou not that Peter, James, yea, Barnabas himself, did rebuke him and withdrew from his way?{{fn:<strong>Editorial note.</strong> The canonical texts record the reverse in the case of Peter: Paul of Tarsus rebuked Peter at Antioch after “certain came from James”, and even Barnabas was carried away (Galatians 2:11-14). Acts records a “sharp contention” in which Paul of Tarsus and Barnabas parted company (Acts 15:36-40). The elders at Jerusalem under James pressed Paul of Tarsus to show that he kept the law (Acts 21:18-24). A rebuke of Paul of Tarsus by Peter is not recorded in these sources. See <a href="/paul/james/">James and the Jerusalem church</a>.}} How then art thou turned so swiftly from the teaching of ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ, who never laid claim to godhead for Himself{{fn:Mark 10:18; John 14:28; 17:3. In the Qur’an, 5:116-117 and 4:171-172.}}, unto one who maketh Him a god upon feeble reasoning and false speech?',
				'Try every spirit.{{fn:“Beloved, believe not every spirit, but try the spirits whether they are of God” (1 John 4:1, Authorised Version). Compare “Prove all things” (1 Thessalonians 5:21).}} Weigh each saying, and examine every claim. Be not beguiled by the glitter of sweet words that lead astray, for even the adversary doth disguise himself as an angel of light{{fn:2 Corinthians 11:14, where Paul of Tarsus applies the saying to his own rivals (11:13).}}. And is it not the disguised one who cometh bearing the gospel of Christ Jesus, which heaven hath never confirmed?',
				'Knowest thou not that the tale of the Damascus event, as he told it, is at variance with itself?{{fn:Acts 9:3-9, 22:6-11 and 26:12-18 give three tellings. In 9:7 the companions stand speechless; in 26:14 they all fall to the ground. In 9:7 they hear the voice; in 22:9 they do not. See <a href="/paul/acts-galatians/">Acts against Galatians</a>.}} What then is this? A revelation, or his own contriving? If it came from heaven, would not its course be steadfast? If it were truly from God, Most High and Glorious, would it not sound one note? Or is the holy word mingled with confused speech, until the whole world is bewildered who is in the right and who is corrupt?',
				'I bear witness with all my soul and understanding that the gospel which setteth aside the law, putteth away circumcision{{fn:Galatians 5:2-6; 6:15; 1 Corinthians 7:19. Compare Acts 16:3, where Paul of Tarsus has Timothy circumcised, and Genesis 17:10-14.}}, and exalteth ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ to be Lord Sovereign{{fn:Romans 10:9; 1 Corinthians 8:6; Philippians 2:9-11. In the Qur’an, 3:79-80 and 5:72-75.}}, is no gospel of heaven. If ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ Himself hath said, “I am come not to destroy the law,”{{fn:Matthew 5:17, in the Authorised Version: “Think not that I am come to destroy the law, or the prophets: I am not come to destroy, but to fulfil.”}} how then saith Paul of Tarsus, “Ye are not under the law”?{{fn:Galatians 5:18; compare Romans 6:14 and Galatians 3:25. Christian interpreters read these of the law as a means of justification. See <a href="/paul/paul-against-jesus/">Paul of Tarsus against Jesus</a>.}} Do two voices proceed from one Lord, or hath enmity arisen among them that wrote?',
				'Brother, I say not this to cast thee down, but that thou mayest awake. Hast thou searched out whom thou followest? If ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ never taught godhead for Himself, and yet thou dost worship Him, who then hath turned thy worship aside? Dost thou not tremble, lest thou stand one day before God, Most High and Glorious, and perceive that he whom thou didst follow was one who was never sent, yet styled himself a servant of Christ Jesus?',
				'I write with a flood of tears{{fn:Paul of Tarsus speaks of his own tears in the same way: Acts 20:19, 31; 2 Corinthians 2:4; Philippians 3:18.}}: forsake Paul of Tarsus, and return unto the true teaching of ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ. Follow Him as a servant of God, Most High and Glorious, and not as a Lord Sovereign lifted up by the tongues of men. Worship Him alone who is One{{fn:Deuteronomy 6:4; Mark 12:29. In the Qur’an, 112:1-4.}}, and not him whom words and the devices of men have made a god.',
				'Carry my greeting to all that seek the truth in the true ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ. Hold fast to the oneness of God{{fn:“Hold fast” is Paul of Tarsus’s own phrase for steadfastness (1 Thessalonians 5:21; 2 Thessalonians 2:15).}}, and shun the gospel that came not from heaven, nor from an apostle, nor from ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ, but from one who repeateth the name of Christ Jesus as a shield for his error.',
				'This letter have I written with mine own hand{{fn:Paul of Tarsus closes letters in his own hand as a mark of authenticity (1 Corinthians 16:21; Galatians 6:11; Colossians 4:18; 2 Thessalonians 3:17).}}, as a token that I live no more in the shadow of Paul of Tarsus.',
				'The grace of God, Most High and Glorious, who sent ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ as His servant{{fn:The benediction follows Galatians 6:18 and Philippians 4:23: “The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you.”}}, and not as a Lord Sovereign held on high, be with thee that seekest the truth. And mayest thou be loosed from that shadow, and return unto the teaching of the true messenger. Amen.',
				'I, who aforetime walked in the shadow of Paul of Tarsus, am now turned unto ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ, peace be upon him, servant of the true Lord Sovereign.',
				'## About this chapter',
				'This chapter is an English rendering, in the manner of the Authorised Version, of the Malay letter <em>Epistel Kepada Jemaat Paulus</em> (“An Epistle unto the Congregation of Paul of Tarsus”). It follows the form of the Pauline letters: salutation, thanksgiving, exhortation, an autograph line, and a benediction. It states the Islamic position and is set apart from the chapters above as a bonus. For the evidence behind its claims, see <a href="/paul/acts-galatians/">Acts against Galatians</a> and <a href="/paul/james/">James and the Jerusalem church</a>.',
